      "seed": 926160,
      "version": 11,
      "versionNonce": 2023010614,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1785951370680,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "STAFF CLOSING\n- OAuth establishes identity and delegated authority. Scoped tools + policy narrow what the assistant can do.\n- Durable jobs execute creative work; idempotency makes retries safe; signed webhooks signal completion.\n- Tenant-aware asset authorization prevents leakage, while append-only audit and lineage make every agent action explainable.",
      "fontSize": 19,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "middle",
      "containerId": "e135",
      "originalText": "STAFF CLOSING\n- OAuth establishes identity and delegated authority. Scoped tools + policy narrow what the assistant can do.\n- Durable jobs execute creative work; idempotency makes retries safe; signed webhooks signal completion.\n- Tenant-aware asset authorization prevents leakage, while append-only audit and lineage make every agent action explainable.",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "HbbCEgximSVd6e0AmjGU9",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 1416.0043953621607,
      "y": 145.48706594551342,
      "width": 1047.198486328125,
      "height": 220,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b1C",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 2066501290,
      "version": 41,
      "versionNonce": 399348394,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1785950892013,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "* OAuth Authorization Code, PKCE, server-to-server credentials, token vaulting, revocation, and delegated authorization\n* Tenant isolation across identity, databases, caches, queues, storage, encryption, webhooks, and observability\n* Narrow, versioned, policy-enforced tools with approval gates\n* Long-running job state machines, cancellation, worker leases, retries, reconciliation, and deterministic outputs\n* Submission and downstream-effect idempotency\n* Signed CloudEvents-style webhooks, retries, deduplication, replay, DLQs, filtering, and SSRF protection\n* Hierarchical rate limits, quotas, cost reservation, weighted fair queues, and noisy-neighbor controls\n* Asset ACL checks, signed URLs, classifications, quarantine states, TOCTOU reauthorization, and lineage\n* Append-only auditing, tamper evidence, SIEM integration, and correlation IDs\n* API contracts, /v1 versioning, independent tool versions, deprecation headers, and migration strategy\n",
      "fontSize": 16,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "* OAuth Authorization Code, PKCE, server-to-server credentials, token vaulting, revocation, and delegated authorization\n* Tenant isolation across identity, databases, caches, queues, storage, encryption, webhooks, and observability\n* Narrow, versioned, policy-enforced tools with approval gates\n* Long-running job state machines, cancellation, worker leases, retries, reconciliation, and deterministic outputs\n* Submission and downstream-effect idempotency\n* Signed CloudEvents-style webhooks, retries, deduplication, replay, DLQs, filtering, and SSRF protection\n* Hierarchical rate limits, quotas, cost reservation, weighted fair queues, and noisy-neighbor controls\n* Asset ACL checks, signed URLs, classifications, quarantine states, TOCTOU reauthorization, and lineage\n* Append-only auditing, tamper evidence, SIEM integration, and correlation IDs\n* API contracts, /v1 versioning, independent tool versions, deprecation headers, and migration strategy\n",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "nrKjRTMENoQGNbA5D9uJw",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 87.67135932074132,
      "y": -652.1633599464841,
      "width": 1362.264864107543,
      "height": 745.1097044864354,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b1D",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 642459434,
      "version": 330,
      "versionNonce": 433291946,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1785951277162,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "1. User prompt\nThe user asks the host application, such as ChatGPT, Copilot, or an Adobe surface, to perform a creative task.\n\n2. Host application\nThe host sends the conversation, available tool schemas, and relevant context to the LLM. It does not send reusable Adobe credentials to the model.\n\n3. LLM planning\nThe LLM decides whether it can answer directly or should call a tool. For a creative action, it emits a structured MCP tool call such as design.create_from_template.\n\n4. MCP client\nThe host’s MCP client validates the tool name and arguments, attaches the user/session identity and opaque Adobe connection ID, and sends the request to the MCP server.\n\n5. MCP server\nThe MCP server is the controlled boundary between the model and business APIs. It:\n\n* exposes only approved tools,\n* validates schemas,\n* resolves tenant and user identity,\n* checks OAuth scopes,\n* checks tenant policy and asset permissions,\n* requests user approval for sensitive actions,\n* enforces idempotency and rate limits,\n* records audit events.\n\n6. Auth broker\nThe MCP server exchanges the opaque connection ID for a short-lived Adobe access token. Refresh tokens remain encrypted in a token vault and are never exposed to the LLM.\n\n7. API gateway\nThe request enters the Adobe integration gateway, which performs authentication, tenant routing, throttling, request tracing, version validation, and admission control.\n\n8. Workflow orchestrator\nFast read operations may call a downstream service synchronously. Creative generation, rendering, bulk edits, video, or export operations become durable asynchronous jobs.\n\n9. Domain services\nTypical services include:\n\n* Template service: search and retrieve permitted templates.\n* Design service: create documents and apply edits.\n* Asset service: resolve assets, enforce ACLs, and issue signed URLs.\n* Generation service: invoke Firefly or other model workers.\n* Export service: render PNG, PDF, MP4, or other output formats.\n* Moderation service: validate prompts, inputs, and generated outputs.\n* Brand policy service: enforce approved fonts, colors, logos, and templates.\n\n10. Job infrastructure\nThe orchestrator writes the job to a database, publishes it through an outbox to a durable queue, and workers process it. Workers checkpoint progress, retry transient failures, and use deterministic operation keys to avoid duplicate effects.\n\n11. Storage\nMetadata is stored in a transactional database. Binary inputs and outputs are stored in object storage. Approved outputs are served through short-lived signed CDN URLs.\n\n12. Completion\nThe job service updates the final state and emits an event. A webhook dispatcher sends a signed completion event to the host. The host can also poll or subscribe for status.\n\n13. Tool result to LLM\nThe MCP server converts the internal response into a minimal structured result containing the job ID, status, preview URLs, and next allowed actions.\n\n14. Final response\nThe LLM turns the tool result into a user-facing response, such as: “Your design is ready,” with preview and open-in-Adobe actions.",
      "fontSize": 10.27737523429566,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "1. User prompt\nThe user asks the host application, such as ChatGPT, Copilot, or an Adobe surface, to perform a creative task.\n\n2. Host application\nThe host sends the conversation, available tool schemas, and relevant context to the LLM. It does not send reusable Adobe credentials to the model.\n\n3. LLM planning\nThe LLM decides whether it can answer directly or should call a tool. For a creative action, it emits a structured MCP tool call such as design.create_from_template.\n\n4. MCP client\nThe host’s MCP client validates the tool name and arguments, attaches the user/session identity and opaque Adobe connection ID, and sends the request to the MCP server.\n\n5. MCP server\nThe MCP server is the controlled boundary between the model and business APIs. It:\n\n* exposes only approved tools,\n* validates schemas,\n* resolves tenant and user identity,\n* checks OAuth scopes,\n* checks tenant policy and asset permissions,\n* requests user approval for sensitive actions,\n* enforces idempotency and rate limits,\n* records audit events.\n\n6. Auth broker\nThe MCP server exchanges the opaque connection ID for a short-lived Adobe access token. Refresh tokens remain encrypted in a token vault and are never exposed to the LLM.\n\n7. API gateway\nThe request enters the Adobe integration gateway, which performs authentication, tenant routing, throttling, request tracing, version validation, and admission control.\n\n8. Workflow orchestrator\nFast read operations may call a downstream service synchronously. Creative generation, rendering, bulk edits, video, or export operations become durable asynchronous jobs.\n\n9. Domain services\nTypical services include:\n\n* Template service: search and retrieve permitted templates.\n* Design service: create documents and apply edits.\n* Asset service: resolve assets, enforce ACLs, and issue signed URLs.\n* Generation service: invoke Firefly or other model workers.\n* Export service: render PNG, PDF, MP4, or other output formats.\n* Moderation service: validate prompts, inputs, and generated outputs.\n* Brand policy service: enforce approved fonts, colors, logos, and templates.\n\n10. Job infrastructure\nThe orchestrator writes the job to a database, publishes it through an outbox to a durable queue, and workers process it. Workers checkpoint progress, retry transient failures, and use deterministic operation keys to avoid duplicate effects.\n\n11. Storage\nMetadata is stored in a transactional database. Binary inputs and outputs are stored in object storage. Approved outputs are served through short-lived signed CDN URLs.\n\n12. Completion\nThe job service updates the final state and emits an event. A webhook dispatcher sends a signed completion event to the host. The host can also poll or subscribe for status.\n\n13. Tool result to LLM\nThe MCP server converts the internal response into a minimal structured result containing the job ID, status, preview URLs, and next allowed actions.\n\n14. Final response\nThe LLM turns the tool result into a user-facing response, such as: “Your design is ready,” with preview and open-in-Adobe actions.",
